#
# prima-materia — chaos magic: Hine, Aspects of Evocation
#
# The harvest of one work of the corpus of pmt:ChaosMagic, kept apart from
# traditions/chaos-magic.ttl, which declares the tradition and the work
# (pmw:AspectsOfEvocation); the edition read and the counts of the harvest
# stand as notes on the work there. Nodes are grouped by essay in the order
# of the collection.
#
# Harvested under prima-materia#893 with two independent extraction passes,
# reconciled by offset and word overlap, then integrated by a reader of the
# whole text. Every record was compared with the Hine nodes of
# traditions/chaos-magic-condensed-chaos.ttl,
# traditions/chaos-magic-oven-ready-chaos.ttl,
# traditions/chaos-magic-prime-chaos.ttl and
# traditions/chaos-magic-pseudonomicon.ttl: where an earlier Hine node
# carries the same claim, no node is written here; a node on the same topic
# in another form carries a note beginning "Differs from". Where Hine
# reports another author whose work is reachable, the node holds his
# ascription. Signs of success, outcome reports and conditions of failure
# carry a note beginning "Counted as".
#


#
# Introduction
#

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEssayTheoryBeforePractice a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Yog-Sothoth essay is theory written ahead of practice"@en, "Yog-Sothoth-Essay als Theorie vor der Praxis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author states that the final essay is an attempt at a theoretical model, written at a period when he liked to build models before starting practical projects; it is therefore not reported as a tested method."@en ;
    skos:note "A statement about how far the last essay of the collection was tested when it was written."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Introduction, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEvocationNotBlackMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation is not black magic"@en, "Evokation ist keine Schwarze Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Against occultists who tie evocation to raising demons and label it black magic (a view the author links to Dennis Wheatley novels), the author holds it to be a very practical, useful set of techniques; he credits the eclectic Chaos Magic movement with loosening the old dogmas."@en ;
    skos:note "Opponent is an unnamed group of occultists; Wheatley named as the source of their image."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBEvocationNeitherLesserNorWrong (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node rejects the views that task evocation is lesser, unspiritual magic and that magicians ought not to command spirits; here, in the introduction of 1998, the target is the occultists who equate evocation with raising demons and with black magic, an image traced to Dennis Wheatley, and the loosening of that dogma is credited to the eclectic turn that chaos magic helped bring about."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Introduction, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .



#
# Howling
#

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeBabblogueRetirement a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Babblogue (personal magical retirement)"@en, "Das Babblogue (persönliche magische Klausur)"@de ;
    skos:definition "A solitary magical retirement in which the senses are deliberately thrown into disorder and a private din is orchestrated, so as to descend into the subconscious and confront and bind the inner lurkers. Steps in the account: compile a self-catalogue (black book, self-portraits, book of blunders); turn extracts into cut-up tapes; arrange seclusion, food and a junk-filled black temple; drive gnosis by overload, fasting and sleeplessness; flood awareness with images and give them form as psychographs; name the demons that emerge; then bind them with the resulting alphabet into magical weapons."@en ;
    skos:note "Presented as a personal account and not as a prescription; the introduction says it was inspired by accounts of Abra-Melin retirements and shaped by Austin Osman Spare and Industrial art."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicOrcBabblogue (Oven-Ready Chaos, text of 1992): the Oven-Ready node gives the Babblogue as a definition; here the retirement as a whole is the practice, and this version follows it with a Commentary (Maslow, the shaman's journey) that names its yields, while the six months of banishing recurring moods told in Oven-Ready Chaos are absent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeBindingInnerDemons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Confronting and binding inner demons"@en, "Konfrontation und Bindung innerer Dämonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ability to bring the hidden drives of one's own psyche to the surface, face them, and bind them."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCDemonBinding (Condensed Chaos, 1995): there personal demons in general can be recognised, bound and turned to work for one; here binding is the aim of one solitary retirement of deliberate sensory derangement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

