pmc:ChaosMagicAoeMaslowLowerNeedsFirstPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Lower needs before higher ones"@en, "Niedrigere Bedürfnisse vor höheren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Per Maslow, the needs of one level must be partly satisfied before the next level matters; aesthetic needs recede when one is starving."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from Abraham Maslow; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicAoeMaslowBiologicalNeeds ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicAoeMaslowAestheticNeeds ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The higher level does not become important while the lower stays unmet: aesthetic needs are said to count for little when one is starving."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Hierarchy of Human Needs, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSurvivalDemonPossessionCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Possession by survival demons"@en, "Besessenheit durch Überlebensdämonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Survival demons can possess a person, who may then do things otherwise unthinkable; cannibalism by air-crash survivors is the extreme example."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicAoeMaslowBiologicalNeeds ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Hierarchy of Human Needs, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRussianDollDemons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Russian-doll structure of demons"@en, "Matroschka-Struktur der Dämonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A cognitive value has roots that can be traced down into the layers of esteem, affiliation and survival needs."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondCDemonDialogue (Condensed Chaos, 1995): there a demon is peeled layer by layer in dialogue to show what it does to keep one as one is; here the layering is a finding of the retirement: a cognitive value has roots running down through esteem and affiliation into survival needs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Hierarchy of Human Needs, page unmarked in text layer; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeDenyingOnesDemonsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Denying one's demons"@en, "Verleugnung der eigenen Dämonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Denied demons stand outside and turn the self into a fortress at war with itself; the Babblogue aims instead at understanding and unifying the inner dwellers through catharsis."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicCondCPersonalDemons ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Hierarchy of Human Needs, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeUnifyingDwellersWithin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unifying the dwellers within"@en, "Vereinigung der inneren Bewohner"@de ;
    skos:definition "Understanding and integrating one's inner entities instead of suppressing them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Hierarchy of Human Needs, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeBabblogueYieldsUnifyingDwellers a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Babblogue yields integration by catharsis"@en, "Babblogue bewirkt Integration durch Katharsis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The retirement is called a trial by catharsis whose aim is to understand and unify the inner dwellers."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeBabblogueRetirement ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeUnifyingDwellersWithin ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Hierarchy of Human Needs, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneySeries a pm:Sequencing ;
    rdfs:label "The shaman's journey"@en, "Die Reise des Schamanen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The inner journey that Hine calls the core theme of every magical retirement of this kind and of the strongest religious myths: a descent into chaos, with its meeting with death, ordeals and feast of demons and its communion with the dead, and then a return, set out as departure, initiation, illumination or transformation, return and mastery."@en ;
    skos:note "Joseph Campbell, The Masks of God, stands in the Further Reading of the essay; the text itself names no source for the scheme."@en ;
    rdf:_1 pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyDeparture ;
    rdf:_2 pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyInitiation ;
    rdf:_3 pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyIllumination ;
    rdf:_4 pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyReturn ;
    rdf:_5 pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyMastery ;
    pm:seriesStatement "Hine sums up the core of the shaman's inward journey as one process in a fixed order: departure, initiation, illumination or transformation, return, mastery. Each element takes its sense from its place, since the ordeals of initiation come only after the separation and descent, and the return and mastery only after the transformation." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyDeparture a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Phase of Departure"@en, "Phase des Aufbruchs"@de ;
    skos:definition "First element of the inner journey: the call to set out, separation from everyday life, and the descent."@en ;
    skos:note "Member 1 of pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneySeries."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyInitiation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Phase of Initiation"@en, "Phase der Initiation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Second element: the ordeals, the labyrinth, the womb and the belly of the whale, with guides and allies met there."@en ;
    skos:note "Member 2 of pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneySeries."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyIllumination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Illumination or transformation"@en, "Erleuchtung oder Verwandlung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Third element, printed on a line of its own between initiation and return: illumination, or transformation."@en ;
    skos:note "Member 3 of pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneySeries."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyReturn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Phase of Return"@en, "Phase der Rückkehr"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fourth element: rebirth and coming back to the world."@en ;
    skos:note "Member 4 of pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneySeries."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneyMastery a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mastery"@en, "Meisterschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fifth and last element, printed on a line of its own after the return: mastery, the initiate back among human affairs with realised power."@en ;
    skos:note "Member 5 of pmc:ChaosMagicAoeShamansJourneySeries."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeChaosMagickRemovingBeliefStructures a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos Magick as removal of belief structures"@en, "Chaosmagie als Abbau von Glaubensstrukturen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Awareness of the journey-process is said to be central to Chaos Magick, which examines and removes the beliefs and cultural conditioning that shape experience; deliberate self-wounding serves a return to a felt lost union with Cosmos and Chaos."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcABeliefStructuresRealityBeliefsTools (Prime Chaos, 1999): there chaos magic is defined by the claim that belief shapes reality and beliefs are tools; here by awareness of the descent-and-return process and by examining and removing the belief structures of cultural conditioning, a deliberate self-wounding."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeInnerJourneyYieldsSelfDeterminingSurvival a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Inner journey yields survival ability"@en, "Innere Reise bewirkt Überlebensfähigkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The benefit of the self-wounding journey is said to be this increased ability to survive."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeBabblogueRetirement ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSelfDeterminingSurvival ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSelfDeterminingSurvival a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Increased ability to survive as self-determining agent"@en, "Gesteigerte Überlebensfähigkeit als selbstbestimmter Akteur"@de ;
    skos:definition "Not by fighting or passively accepting circumstances but by grasping that self and surroundings are basically one and how far one can determine oneself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Howling, Commentary, The Shaman's Journey, p. 11; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .



#
# GoHu Servitor
#

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGossipHunterSeekerKillerNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Gossip Hunter-Seeker-Killer"@en, "Klatsch-Jäger-Sucher-Töter"@de ;
    skos:definition "A servitor the author began building to patrol the astral, detect gossip about him and fire off a missile carrying a nasty runic curse; he tested it with an empty missile at an unsuspecting colleague and then dropped the project."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Gossip Hunter-Seeker-Killer" ;
    pm:nameRole "servitor, begun and abandoned; meant to curse gossipers" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 19; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeAttackServitorOverkillCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Self-warning: an attack servitor was overkill"@en, "Selbstwarnung: ein Angriffs-Servitor war übertrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "After test-firing a blank at a colleague the author recognised that a cursing anti-gossip servitor was going too far, born of paranoia and anger, and reframed the problem instead."@en ;
    skos:note "Self-warning of the author against his own first design."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGossipHunterSeekerKillerNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 19; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGossipAsEnergyAndPublicity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gossip as energy and publicity"@en, "Klatsch als Energie und Öffentlichkeitsarbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "People who talk about you behind your back may be said to feed you energy; being talked about beats obscurity, rumours do free PR, and some may even act as an enchantment without effort."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 19; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGoHuNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "GoHu"@en, "GoHu"@de ;
    skos:definition "A servitor whose task was to get people talking about the author, generate rumours and make sure he eventually heard of them; since some gossip already existed it acted chiefly as an amplifier and as a receiver beaming rumours back to him. Called one of his most successful servitors."@en ;
    skos:note "The essay heading is misread as Gotu in the text layer; the running text reads GoHu."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBGoHuNaming (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 GoHu is a dark, slightly hollowed mirror into which sigilised desires are sent and which gives off ideas before divination; the GoHu of this essay is a servitor that stirs up and amplifies gossip about its maker and beams it back to him. Same name, a different servitor in each text."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "GoHu" ;
    pm:nameRole "servitor that generates and relays rumour about its maker" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 20; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeGoHuRumourServitor a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Making a servitor to generate rumour about oneself"@en, "Einen Servitor schaffen, der Gerüchte über einen selbst erzeugt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Create a servitor whose task is to get people talking about its maker, start rumours, amplify the gossip already going round and see that the maker hears of it in the end; the author made it after dropping an attack servitor, on the view that being talked about feeds one and does free publicity. No design or launch details are given."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it as a node of its own. The yielding of record A116 names no practice; the task is stated in the text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 20; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRumourAmplification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Amplifying and relaying rumours about oneself"@en, "Verstärken und Zuleiten von Gerüchten über sich selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Encouraging gossip about the operator, magnifying what exists and channelling reports back to him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 21; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGoHuYieldsRumourAmplification a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "GoHu yields rumour amplification"@en, "GoHu bewirkt Gerüchteverstärkung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The servitor amplified and returned rumours."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeGoHuRumourServitor ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRumourAmplification ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, p. 21; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGoHuRumoursOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rumours that reached the author after GoHu"@en, "Gerüchte, die nach GoHu den Autor erreichten"@de ;
    skos:definition "After activation six rumours came back: that he owned a castle in southern France, owned a London occult bookshop, was secretly Peter J. Carroll, was bidding for a Birmingham Goth club (someone phoned to check), had a sexual relationship with a named Chaos International editor, and was a blood-drinking vampire (printed in two books, traced to a first-person vampire story he wrote)."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: six rumours reached the author after GoHu went active (a castle in France, the Atlantis Bookshop, being Peter J. Carroll, a Goth club in Birmingham, a sexual rumour, vampirism printed in two books); he also calls GoHu one of his most successful servitors. Rumour about him was already circulating before the servitor, as he says himself, and nothing is given that separates the servitor's rumours from those that would have come anyway."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), GoHu Servitor, The Rumours, p. 21; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .



#
# Functional Spirits
#

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeSummoningSpiritInItsSituation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Summoning a spirit on the spot in its situation"@en, "Spontanes Herbeirufen eines Geistes in seiner Situation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Instead of formal ritual, summon the relevant spirit at the moment you find yourself in a situation under its governance."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondBSituationalSummoningGoflowolfog (Condensed Chaos, 1995): there the summoning of one spirit in a jam; here the general alternative to triangle conjuration, calling a functional spirit at the moment one is in its situation, which the introduction says needs no ritual trappings at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Functional Spirits, p. 22; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .



#
# Some Observations from the Goetia Project
#

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGoetiaProject a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Goetia Project"@en, "Das Goetia-Projekt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A long-term project begun in 1989 by the author and a colleague: go through several traditional grimoires in turn, assess and analyse the findings, and devise a general Goetic approach fitted to a post-modern age; the author admits it did not go as planned."@en ;
    skos:note "The author says that things did not go as planned; no condition of the deviation is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeVassagoNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Vassago"@en, "Vassago"@de ;
    skos:definition "A Lesser Key spirit, the first evoked in the project. Asked the best time to evoke him, he showed a sundial, hourglass and skull; tested for a remote vision of the colleague's whereabouts next afternoon, he gave an accurate picture of corridors, lockers and stairs at a polytechnic that blurred at the computer room. The pair concluded computers, like digital watches, lie outside his experience."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Vassago" ;
    pm:nameRole "spirit in the Lesser Key (Goetia)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEvocationWithoutCircleFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation without circle and triangle"@en, "Evokation ohne Kreis und Dreieck"@de ;
    skos:definition "Their first evocation of Vassago, done without circle and triangle just to see, was poorly defined: the spirit appeared only hazily at best, and both operators felt drained, had headaches and flu-like symptoms lasting two days or so."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of an evocation done without circle and triangle: the working lacked definition, the spirit was hazy at best, and both operators were drained, with headaches and pre-flu symptoms for some days. No deadline; the cause is put on the omitted traditional arrangement, and the pair changed their method in response."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeUnprotectedEvocationAfterEffectsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Health effects of unprotected evocation"@en, "Gesundheitsfolgen ungeschützter Evokation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Working without the traditional circle and triangle was followed by a drop in vitality, headaches and pre-flu symptoms for days."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEvocationWithoutCircleFailure ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeCircleTriangleBeforeLesserKeyPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Circle and triangle before Lesser Key evocation"@en, "Kreis und Dreieck vor Evokationen des Lesser Key"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the failed first working they built a permanent circle and triangle by the Lesser Key's rules and conducted all further workings in its prescribed fashion."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicAoePermanentCircleAndTriangle ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeyConjurationAndConstraint ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without them the working lacked definition, visions stayed hazy, and both operators were drained with headaches and pre-flu symptoms for days."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoePermanentCircleAndTriangle a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Permanent circle and triangle after the Lesser Key"@en, "Dauerhafter Kreis und Dreieck nach dem Lesser Key"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the first evocation without them failed, build a lasting circle and triangle by the Lesser Key's rules and work every later evocation in the manner that book sets out."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it as a node of its own. Record A150 names this step as its prior without a node for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeyConjurationAndConstraint a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Lesser Key conjuration and constraint"@en, "Beschwörung und Bannung nach dem Lesser Key"@de ;
    skos:definition "Recite the Lesser Key's lengthy conjurations and constraints, full of Old Testament divine names and Bible quotations, until excitement reaches fever pitch; once the spirit is discernible, abjure it to stay inside the Triangle of Art."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcBBasicTwoPhaseEvocation (Prime Chaos, 1999): there a general two-phase scheme in which the Lesser Key triangle is one option; here the long conjurations as the two operators worked them, the excitement they raise, and the charge to stay in the triangle once the spirit is perceptible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeFeverPitchExcitement a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Exhilaration raised to fever pitch"@en, "Erregung bis zum Fieberpunkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The long conjurations quickly drive excitement to a peak."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeyConjurationsYieldExcitement a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "LK conjurations yield fever-pitch excitement and visible spirit"@en, "LK-Beschwörungen bewirken höchste Erregung und sichtbaren Geist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The conjurations raise excitement and bring the spirit to discernibility."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeyConjurationAndConstraint ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeFeverPitchExcitement ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSpiritDiscernibleSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit becomes discernible"@en, "Geist wird wahrnehmbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "The spirit becoming perceptible signals that the conjuration has taken and it is time to constrain it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: that the spirit becomes perceptible, after which it is to be charged to stay in the triangle. Judged by the operators alone; the text gives no mark of perceptibility beyond the visions themselves."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSpiritFormsSettle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit forms close to but not exactly as in the Lesser Key; then stable"@en, "Geistgestalten ähnlich, nicht gleich dem Lesser Key; danach beständig"@de ;
    skos:definition "The spirits rarely took exactly the shapes the Lesser Key gives, though generally similar ones; once a spirit had shown a form, it kept roughly that form in later evocations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeQuestioningEvokedSpirit a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Questioning the evoked spirit"@en, "Befragen des evozierten Geistes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once a spirit is present, question it about its nature and qualities and about how to improve the evocation environment (e.g. the best time to evoke it)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 25; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeRemoteVisionTestOfSpirit a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Testing a spirit with a checkable remote vision"@en, "Prüfen eines Geistes durch eine überprüfbare Fernschau"@de ;
    skos:definition "Require the spirit to show one operator where the other will be at a set later time, then compare with the other's report."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRemoteVisionFromSpirit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Remote/precognitive vision via Vassago"@en, "Fern- bzw. Vorausschau durch Vassago"@de ;
    skos:definition "A vision of a colleague's location the following afternoon, given by the spirit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeVassagoTestYieldsRemoteVision a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Testing Vassago yields a remote vision"@en, "Prüfung Vassagos bewirkt eine Fernschau"@de ;
    skos:definition "The demand produced an accurate vision of the colleague's route."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeRemoteVisionTestOfSpirit ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRemoteVisionFromSpirit ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeVassagoVisionConfirmedOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vassago's vision confirmed"@en, "Vassagos Vision bestätigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The colleague later confirmed the building seen was the place he had been."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: the colleague later said the building seen was Leeds Polytechnic, where he had been. The checking was by the colleague's own report after the vision; no record of the vision made before the check is mentioned."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeVisionBlursAtComputersFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vision blurs at the computer room"@en, "Vision verschwimmt im Computerraum"@de ;
    skos:definition "The vision went unclear exactly at the room of computers, which the pair read as computers (and digital watches) being outside Vassago's experience."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of the remote-vision test: the vision went unclear at the room of computers, which the pair read as lying outside the spirit's experience. The cause is put on the spirit's range, not on the operators."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeGodFormInvocationBeforeEvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking a god-form before evocation"@en, "Anrufung einer Gottesform vor der Evokation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before starting the evocation, invoke a suitable god-form such as Horus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGodFormBeforeConjurationPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "God-form invocation before conjuration"@en, "Gottesform-Anrufung vor der Beschwörung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The invocation is placed before the evocatory process."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicAoeGodFormInvocationBeforeEvocation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeyConjurationAndConstraint ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEasierConjuration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Easier conjuration of certain spirits"@en, "Leichtere Beschwörung bestimmter Geister"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prior god-form invocation seemed to ease the conjuring of spirits like Haures or Andromalius."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGodFormInvocationYieldsEasierConjuration a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "God-form invocation yields easier conjuration"@en, "Gottesform-Anrufung erleichtert die Beschwörung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invoking Horus first seemed to make some conjurations easier."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeGodFormInvocationBeforeEvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEasierConjuration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeHauresAndromaliusNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Haures and Andromalius"@en, "Haures und Andromalius"@de ;
    skos:definition "Lesser Key spirits whose conjuration seemed easier after invoking a god-form such as Horus."@en ;
    skos:note "Andromelius as printed; the Lesser Key spells the name Andromalius."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Haures; Andromelius" ;
    pm:nameRole "spirits in the Lesser Key (Goetia)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGlasyaLabolasNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Glasya-Labolas"@en, "Glasya-Labolas"@de ;
    skos:definition "Lesser Key spirit whose manifestation kept flickering into and out of view in the triangle while chaos vortices were open, and came through once they were closed."@en ;
    skos:note "Glasyglabolas as printed; the Lesser Key spells the name Glasya-Labolas."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Glasyglabolas" ;
    pm:nameRole "spirit in the Lesser Key (Goetia)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeChaosVorticesImpedeManifestationFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos invocation impeded manifestation"@en, "Chaos-Anrufung behinderte die Manifestation"@de ;
    skos:definition "After preceding the conjuration with a chaos invocation, The Unsealing of the Vortices (written for Eris workings), a windstorm rose round the house and the spirit only flickered in the triangle; closing the vortices calmed the wind and let it come through."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of a conjuration preceded by a chaos invocation: the spirit kept appearing and vanishing in the triangle while the vortices were open and came through once they were closed. The cause is put on the energies of the opened vortices."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeChaosEnergiesBlockGoeticSpiritsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos energies may block Lesser Key spirits"@en, "Chaos-Energien können Lesser-Key-Geister blockieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The energies raised by an opened chaos invocation seemed to hinder the spirit's manifestation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeyConjurationAndConstraint ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeLesserKeySpiritsConservative a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lesser Key spirits are conservative about conditions"@en, "Lesser-Key-Geister sind konservativ bei den Bedingungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Such incidents led the pair to suppose the Lesser Key spirits are fairly conservative about the parameters in which they can be called; their advice on ideal conditions belonged to a baroque school (cellars, thick incense, much ritual equipment)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Some Observations from the Goetia Project, p. 26; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .



#
# Evoking Yog-Sothoth
#

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeTangibleEvocationOfOldOnesAim a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Aim: a method to evoke Great Old Ones tangibly"@en, "Ziel: eine Methode, Große Alte greifbar zu evozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Esoteric Order of Dagon seeks effective techniques for dealing with the Great Old Ones; the essay proposes a possible methodology for contacting entities like Yog-Sothoth by combining research from several fields with the Cthulhu Mythos."@en ;
    skos:note "Offered as a possible methodology, not as a tested one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeLovecraftFictionAsGuide a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lovecraft's fiction as primary guide"@en, "Lovecrafts Fiktion als Hauptleitfaden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rather than approaching Lovecraftian themes through traditional systems such as Qabalah (which may still offer a parallel), the author looks to the fiction itself for guidelines; later he calls Lovecraft's dream-inspired fiction a legitimate source for magical ideas."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCCthulhuMythosCompleteMagicalBelief (Prime Chaos, 1999): there the Mythos is a complete magical system that resists being fitted to the Qabalah; here Lovecraft's fiction itself is the place to look for operating guidelines, the Qabalah at most a parallel, and his dream-inspired fiction a legitimate source for magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeHilltopRitesAndGates a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hilltop rites and gates"@en, "Hügelriten und Tore"@de ;
    skos:definition "From The Dunwich Horror: rites on hilltops, tied to stone circles and odd geophysical phenomena, are key to approaching Yog-Sothoth; bringing the Old Ones into our dimension needs a gate such as a wild outdoor site, stone circle, tower or similar power spot, usually with a long local history and folklore of strange manifestations."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from Lovecraft's tale The Dunwich Horror; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGatewaysInLandscape (The Pseudonomicon, 2004): the 2004 node gathers gateways of every kind, including dream, trance, madness and urban wild places; here, read from The Dunwich Horror, hilltop rites at stone circles and places of strange geophysical events, with a local folklore the sceptics dismiss, are the key to Yog-Sothoth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeGateBeforeOldOnesPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A gate is needed to bring the Old Ones through"@en, "Ein Tor ist nötig, um die Alten hereinzuholen"@de ;
    skos:definition "To draw the Great Old Ones over into this dimension, a gate-site is needed."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as a requirement; no consequence of leaving the gate out is given."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicAoeHilltopRitesAndGates ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAoeYogSothothEvocationProposal ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, p. 27; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEarthLights a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Earth Lights"@en, "Erdlichter"@de ;
    skos:definition "After Paul Devereux: the Earth naturally produces light-forms, often regional, read by witnesses as UFOs or ghosts; they may seem to follow cars or act intelligently; their source is hypothesised as tectonic stress along fault lines; sacred sites often lie on faulted ground and may have amplified and focused the lights; much folklore (will-o'-the-wisps, faeries, ghosts, UFOs) may relate to them."@en ;
    skos:note "Regional-manifestation cases from John Keel (Mothman, Ilkley Moor) cited in support; recorded once as the explanatory model."@en, "Hine reports this from Paul Devereux (printed Deveraux), Earth Lights Revelation; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEncounterPhenomenaCommonFeatures (The Pseudonomicon, 2004): the 2004 node lists features common to encounters (sounds, time distortion, smells, visions); here Devereux's model itself: light-forms produced by tectonic stress along faults, regional, seemingly guided, and sacred sites placed on faulted ground."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, p. 28; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeToroidalInformationStructures a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Toroidal information structures (Fra. Choronzon)"@en, "Toroidale Informationsstrukturen (Fra. Choronzon)"@de ;
    skos:definition "After Fra. Choronzon's essay Chaos Invocation: information might be stored in or carried by doughnut-shaped, partly electromagnetic structures that arise naturally from chaos mathematics (smoke rings, Jupiter's Red Spot); such structures could exist within the geomagnetic field and can be produced by spending neurochemical energy, so the physiological gnosis of ritual can produce them."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from Fra. Choronzon, Chaos Invocation; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 29; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeServitorInformationMatrix a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Servitor as information matrix plus energy"@en, "Servitor als Informationsmatrix plus Energie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Creating an elemental servitor means setting down instructions that define its nature, abilities and functions, then projecting energy into this matrix so that the whole entity forms and can act independently of its maker. Evoking such a servitor may bring a toroidal structure into existence that retains information over time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeAgainstPurelyPsychologicalAccount a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against a purely psychological account of servitors"@en, "Gegen eine rein psychologische Deutung von Servitoren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author holds that a merely psychological or subjective explanation cannot account for a servitor's ability to produce results in the physical world according to its maker's intent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeWorkStrengthensServitor a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Work makes a servitor stronger and independent"@en, "Arbeit macht einen Servitor stärker und eigenständig"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the author's and colleagues' experience, the more tasks an elemental servitor is given, the more powerful it becomes at manipulating probabilities, and in time it may develop a character of its own."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBGeneralPurposeServitorsLearningExpert (Condensed Chaos, 1995): there a general servitor seems to get better at its kind of task with each one it is given; here more work makes an elemental servitor more powerful at bending probability and in time lets it grow a character independent of its maker."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeServitorProbabilityManipulation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Probability manipulation by a servitor"@en, "Wahrscheinlichkeitsbeeinflussung durch einen Servitor"@de ;
    skos:definition "A well-used servitor's growing ability to bend probabilities."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeServitorWorkYieldsProbabilityManipulation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Repeated use yields probability manipulation"@en, "Wiederholter Gebrauch bewirkt Wahrscheinlichkeitsbeeinflussung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Giving the servitor more work increases its power over probability."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCondBFeedingServitor ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeServitorProbabilityManipulation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEntitiesOutliveCreatorCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Entities may outlive their maker"@en, "Wesenheiten können ihren Schöpfer überleben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Powerful entities are said sometimes to survive their creator's death."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as an observation, not phrased as a warning."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicAoeServitorInformationMatrix ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSmallSpiritToGod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "From small spirit to god"@en, "Vom kleinen Geist zum Gott"@de ;
    skos:definition "The more people create or feed such an entity, the better its survival and information storage; given time and enough people's ritual energy, a small spirit could grow into what we usually take to be a god."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 30; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeCredibilityEnvelope a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Credibility envelope; consciousness as emergent"@en, "Glaubwürdigkeitshülle; Bewusstsein als emergent"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author declares that consciousness is not purely internal but emerges from our interaction with the biosphere. Faced with something outside consensus reality, people fit it into whatever category strains that reality least (religious being, ghost, alien), drawing on cultural memes stored in the subconscious; conscious belief is not required."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 31; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeHypnoticRecallUnreliableCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hypnotic recall is unreliable"@en, "Hypnotische Erinnerung ist unzuverlässig"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hypnotic recall tends to produce confabulated material matching what investigators expect, drawn from cultural knowledge of such experiences; satanic-abuse survivor reports are cited as rich in lurid detail but vague on names, dates and places."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicAoeCredibilityEnvelope ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 32; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEarthLightsConsciousnessSensitive a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Earth Lights as consciousness-sensitive"@en, "Erdlichter als bewusstseinsempfindlich"@de ;
    skos:definition "Devereux tentatively proposes that the light-forms respond to the observer's mind, explaining reports of lights following people or playing tag."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from Paul Devereux; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 32; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEvocationInformationMatrixBounds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation builds a matrix that bounds the entity"@en, "Evokation baut eine Matrix, die das Wesen begrenzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whether creating a spirit or evoking one from a grimoire, one focuses energy and builds an information matrix; the resulting discrete entity acts only inside the bounds of the information supplied, either its designed traits or the grimoire's description. Every magical psychocosm (Qabalah, Abra-Melin) instructs how its summoned entities behave."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 32; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSpiritsSeekIndividuality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirits seek humans for individuality"@en, "Geister suchen Menschen wegen der Individualität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Traditional theory says spirits like dealing with humans because we lend them an individuality they lack; the author grants this some truth, as our ability to organise information into discrete wholes is key."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 32; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeEnergyFormsRetainStructure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Energy forms keep the structure first given them"@en, "Energieformen behalten die zuerst gegebene Struktur"@de ;
    skos:definition "If an energy form, once structured by an encounter, keeps that structure and feeds it back to later observers, this would explain why people in a regional flap report similar manifestations; this brings us near a working methodology."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, From Butterflies to Beezlebub, p. 33; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeMasksOnEnergyForms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Masks on energy forms; attraction to cohesive structure"@en, "Masken auf Energieformen; Anziehung zu kohärenter Struktur"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because our brains treat everything as discrete, we project masks or personae onto energy forms; such masks may be kept by the energy structures, not through self-aware intelligence but because some energy forms (per systems theory) are drawn to more cohesive structures like the brain's information field or the fields of cars and power lines."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeAlteredStateOfConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Altered state of consciousness (ASC)"@en, "Veränderter Bewusstseinszustand (ASC)"@de ;
    skos:definition "An altered state in which attention can be intensely focused to a single point and projected."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRhythmStructuresConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rhythm and sound structure consciousness"@en, "Rhythmus und Klang strukturieren das Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rhythm carries consciousness along, prompts movement with less effort, becomes a motor attitude that frees attention, is mirrored by brain activity and has strong physiological effects; music structures hallucinogenic journeys and in Voudoun drumming announces particular lwa. Sound imposes structure on experience, especially on time-sense."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCRhythmAndDelivery (Condensed Chaos, 1995): there rhythm is treated for the delivery of invocations and possessed speech; here rhythm and sound are evidence for the evocation model: marching soldiers tire less, music therapy, music steering hallucinogenic journeys, Voudoun drumming, and sound imposing a structure on the sense of time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 35; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSoundPulseTransfersMatrix a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sound pulse transfers the matrix into the toroid"@en, "Klangimpuls überträgt die Matrix in den Toroid"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fra. Choronzon notes a sound pulse sent into the space a toroidal structure occupies as the means of transferring the information matrix to it."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from Fra. Choronzon, Chaos Invocation; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeForcefulVocalisation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Forceful vocalisation (calls, primal speech, barbarous words)"@en, "Kraftvolle Stimmgebung (Rufe, Ursprache, barbarische Worte)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Forcefully enunciate Enochian Calls, Primal Speech or the Barbarous Words of Evocation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeForcefulVocalisationYieldsASC a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Forceful vocalisation yields ASC"@en, "Kraftvolle Stimmgebung bewirkt ASC"@de ;
    skos:definition "Such vocal techniques are said necessarily to produce an altered state via hyperventilation, increased brain activity and racing heart."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeForcefulVocalisation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeAlteredStateOfConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeFocusingAwarenessOnVector a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Focusing awareness and projecting it"@en, "Ausrichten und Aussenden der Aufmerksamkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "What matters is focusing awareness along one vector and entering an ASC in which attention is concentrated to a single point and sent forth; for advanced practitioners vocalisation is held unnecessary, since sound carries thought."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 36; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeUltrasoundAtMegalithsUnconfirmed a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ultrasound at megaliths unconfirmed"@en, "Ultraschall an Megalithen unbestätigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Devereux mentions possible ultrasound emission at some megalith sites, but the finding is elusive and needs corroboration."@en ;
    skos:note "Devereux himself is said to call the finding elusive and in need of corroboration."@en, "Hine reports this from Paul Devereux, Places of Power; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeTimeProductOfConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Time as a product of consciousness"@en, "Zeit als Erzeugnis des Bewusstseins"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prompted by Devereux's time-slip reports near radiation anomalies at sacred sites, the author suggests time is not an external force but a product of consciousness; in gnosis, time stopping or past, present and future seeming simultaneous is common, as in abduction reports and possession states."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcAMagicTakesPlaceOutsideTime (Prime Chaos, 1999): there each magical act is a script for a momentary slip out of consensus time; here time itself is proposed as a product of consciousness, from Devereux's time-slip reports at sacred sites and from the stopped time of gnosis, abduction reports and possession."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeProjectingAtPeakOfASC a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Projecting will at the peak of the ASC"@en, "Willensaussendung am Höhepunkt des ASC"@de ;
    skos:definition "Release energy to realise your will at the peak of the altered state, where time seems to stop."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRealisingWill a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Realising one's will"@en, "Verwirklichung des eigenen Willens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Projected energy bringing about the operator's intent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoePeakProjectionYieldsRealisedWill a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Peak projection yields realisation of will"@en, "Höhepunkt-Aussendung bewirkt Willensverwirklichung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Projecting at the ASC peak is called the most favourable way to realise one's will."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeProjectingAtPeakOfASC ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeRealisingWill ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 37; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoePersingerGeomagnetismAndBrain a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geomagnetism and the brain (Persinger)"@en, "Geomagnetismus und Gehirn (Persinger)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Persinger links some psi to variations of the geomagnetic field; his work is taken to show the brain generates electromagnetic energy and external EM fields affect brain function, making Choronzon's self-ordering EM structures more plausible."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from Michael Persinger; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 38; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeSacredSitesAsPowerSpots a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sacred sites as power spots for ASC contact"@en, "Heilige Stätten als Kraftorte für ASC-Kontakt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Some sacred sites are power spots that predisposed users to an ASC in which they could interact with electromagnetic energy forms, aided by Earth Lights and hallucinogenic plants (Paul Bennett notes such plants grow near sacred sites); sound acts both on participants and on external energy sources."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 38; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeYogSothothNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Yog-Sothoth"@en, "Yog-Sothoth"@de ;
    skos:definition "Great Old One from Lovecraft. Some modern magicians following Kenneth Grant equate him with the demon Choronzon; the Dunwich Horror passage makes him the gate itself and its key and keeper, in whom past, present and future are one (linked by the author to time-slip); in other Mythos tales he is a conglomerate of iridescent globes, i.e. a light-form, linked with freak winds and storms. The author proposes he may be a guide entity, the keeper of an underworld reached via ASCs, able to manifest as electromagnetic phenomena, a window into the unknown dark."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPsnYogSothothNaming (The Pseudonomicon, 2004): the 2004 node records that Hine once saw Yog-Sothoth as a guide entity and now as the outer edge of the fractal surface; here is that earlier view itself: a guardian of an underworld entered through altered states, able to appear as a series of electromagnetic phenomena, a window into the unknown."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Yog-Sothoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "Great Old One of the Cthulhu Mythos; proposed guide and guardian entity" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 38; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeChoronzonNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Choronzon"@en, "Choronzon"@de ;
    skos:definition "Demon evoked by Crowley and Neuburg in the desert (the text says Gobi), equated with Yog-Sothoth by Grant-influenced magicians and seen as a being of scattering or negative chaos."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine reports this from magicians following Kenneth Grant, for the equation with Yog-Sothoth; the node holds his report, and the named work, reachable in its own right, is not read through him."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Choronzon" ;
    pm:nameRole "demon; equated with Yog-Sothoth by some magicians" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 38; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeYogSothothEvocationProposal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Proposed evocation of Yog-Sothoth"@en, "Vorgeschlagene Evokation Yog-Sothoths"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pieced together from the essay (not given as numbered steps): go to a gate-site, i.e. a wild hill region or stone circle on faulted ground with a history of strange manifestations; use barbarous words and forceful sound and frenzied rites to enter an ASC; project at the peak; give the energy form a structure so it coheres as Yog-Sothoth, an interface or persona through which to gather information. The author calls the Dunwich Horror guidelines clear and operationally valid in his view."@en ;
    skos:note "Pieced together from the essay; the steps are not numbered there. Proposed and untested when written: the essay ends by saying that all that remains is to go and evoke."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeAgainstNegativeChoronzonReading a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against reading Yog-Sothoth as negative Choronzon"@en, "Gegen die Deutung Yog-Sothoths als negatives Choronzon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author argues that work on Yog-Sothoth has been held back by the negative link to Choronzon as a dispersing, negative-chaos entity, a link drawn by magicians leaning on Kenneth Grant; chaos dynamics (and EOD notes on the Mandelbrot Set) are proposed as a more positive view."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 39; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeInsightThroughInterfaces a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Insight through created interfaces"@en, "Einsicht durch erschaffene Schnittstellen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Perhaps, by creating interfaces or personae, information can be gleaned and further insight gained into how we interact with the universe."@en ;
    skos:note "Hedged in the text with perhaps."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeYogSothothEvocationYieldsInsight a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Yog-Sothoth evocation may yield insight"@en, "Yog-Sothoth-Evokation kann Einsicht bewirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treating the entity as a window and building personae may yield information and insight."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAoeYogSothothEvocationProposal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAoeInsightThroughInterfaces ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeYogSothothMethodUntested a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Yog-Sothoth method remains theory"@en, "Yog-Sothoth-Methode bleibt Theorie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Having built the theory, the author says only the actual evocation is left to do; the postscript adds the essay mostly reworks other people's research with his own ideas."@en ;
    skos:note "A statement about how far the method is tested."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, The Neuromancer Effect, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeQuestioningThroughHumanInterface a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Questioning an entity through a human interface"@en, "Befragen eines Wesens über ein menschliches Medium"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ask a suitably talented companion to open his mind to the entity so that the operator can question it through him; which the author calls a reasonably standard way to interrogate unfamiliar entities. End by commanding it to depart."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondAQuestioningAnEntity (Condensed Chaos, 1995): there the entity is questioned directly and dismissed; here it is questioned through a companion who opens his mind to it, a procedure the author calls fairly accepted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeStairwellEntityReceiving a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "Words of the stairwell entity"@en, "Worte des Wesens im Treppenhaus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Through the author's partner acting as interface, an entity seen as a black amorphous shape said it came from the ancient hills, had been woken recently by activity at a sacred site, had come to give the author power for some unnamed purpose, would go back screaming to the hills if refused, and named itself Azathoth."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondAQuestioningAnEntity (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node tells of a man-shaped moving shadow on a stairwell, questioned by Hine and a friend, evasive though promising power, and sent away; here the shape is black and amorphous, it is questioned through the friend as interface, it says it comes from the ancient hills and calls itself Azathoth, and the friend needs a banishing afterwards."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "Phil Hine, questioning the entity through his partner as interface" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "an entity seen as a black amorphous shape, which named itself Azathoth" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "At night in the stairwell to the author's flat; both men saw the shape, the author holding his thunderbolt, and his partner, a magician, let it be questioned through him by opening himself to it." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeStairwellAzathothNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Azathoth (stairwell entity)"@en, "Azathoth (Wesen im Treppenhaus)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Name given by the stairwell entity; the author notes it could have come from the interface's own mind, though he knew little of the Mythos, and doubted so powerful a being would wait politely in a stairwell."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAzathothNaming (The Pseudonomicon, 2004): the 2004 node is the Mythos god as Hine works with it; here the name is only what a stairwell entity called itself, which Hine doubts and thinks may have come from the interface's mind."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Azathoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "name given by an entity met in a stairwell" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeNameFromMediumCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Entity names may come from the medium"@en, "Wesensnamen können vom Medium stammen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The name an entity gives through a human interface may originate in that person's mind."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAoeQuestioningThroughHumanInterface ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeNoStraightAnswerFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "No direct answer from the entity"@en, "Keine direkte Antwort vom Wesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The entity stayed evasive about the power it offered and gave no straight answers, so the author dismissed it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of questioning an entity through an interface: no straight answer about the power it offered, so it was told to go. The cause is left open: the entity's evasion or the interface."@en, "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondAQuestioningAnEntity (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node narrates the evasive entity as a practice of questioning and dismissing; here the evasion is counted as the failure of the questioning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeInterfaceAfterEffectsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "After-effects on the interface"@en, "Nachwirkungen beim Medium"@de ;
    skos:definition "The partner who served as interface later needed an intensive banishing, suffering coldness, tight chest pressure, personality displacement and motor spasms; Persinger describes such symptoms as possible side-effects of Earth Light encounters."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAoeQuestioningThroughHumanInterface ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAoeBanishingAffectedInterface a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing performed on an affected interface"@en, "Bannung am betroffenen Medium"@de ;
    skos:definition "Perform an intensive banishing ritual on the person who acted as interface when symptoms follow the contact (ritual itself not described)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 40; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAoeBackstoneCircleCorroborationOutcome a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Backstone circle encounter read as corroboration"@en, "Begegnung am Backstone-Steinkreis als Bestätigung gelesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When he later told the stairwell story, the author learned that two friends from an earth-mysteries group of West Yorkshire had sat a night inside the Backstone circle, just uncovered at the time, and had seen earth lights, small dwarf-like shadows and lines of energy; he finds it strange that an entity claiming to come from a recently disturbed site should match their experience, and says the episode led him to study Earth Mysteries and magic more closely."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as an outcome report, not as a sign announced in advance: a match found after the event between the stairwell entity's claim to come from a newly disturbed site and two friends' night at the newly uncovered Backstone circle; the author calls it strange, not proof."@en, "Reported by Hine at second hand from two friends of the author in an earth-mysteries group of West Yorkshire."@en, "Added in the reconciliation of the two extraction passes: only the second pass carried it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AspectsOfEvocation ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, Aspects of Evocation: Collected Essays (version 1.1, dated March 1988 in the text, introduction signed March 1998), Evoking Yog-Sothoth, Postscript, p. 41; read in the full-text layer of the archive.org item phil-hine-aspects-of-evocation" .
